\par\par \b \uc2  IMF Poland/Zloty -2:  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Allows More Volatility \b0\par\par\uc2 169 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 September 1996\par 17:13\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1996, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  He said the report was suggesting greater flexibility within the existing exchange-rate mechanism. \par \par \uc2  The Polish zloty is pegged to a basket of five Western \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and is allowed to float with a band of plus or minus seven points of the central parity rate. The parity rate is adjusted gradually to devalue the zloty at a rate that is now set at 1% per month. \par \par \uc2  Early this year, when the IMF report was prepared, the National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  frequently on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to keep the zloty from appreciating too quickly against Western currenies. In practice, the bank didn't allow the zloty to strenthen by more than 2.5% against the parity rate. \par \par \uc2  In recent months, however, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has adopted a more hands-off policy, which has resulted in greater zloty volatility. That in turn has discouraged the inflow of short-term speculative capital. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 12-09-96 \par \par \uc2  1513GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011013ds9c07do4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
