\par\par \b \uc2  OUTLOOK-ZLOTY EYES T-BILL TENDER, YIELDS SEEN FLAT. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jan Strupczewski \par \uc2 566 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Mai 1996\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WARSAW, May 24 (Reuter) - The weekly Polish T-bill auction is seen oversubscribed on Monday but analysts expected yields to stay flat because of a likely small foreign interest due to a banking holiday in London. \par \par \uc2  "I expect total demand to be 1.3 to 1.4 billion zlotys at the tender, but foreign interest is likely to be weak because of the holiday in London, which should help keep maximum yields flat," said Bartlomiej Kumela of Societe Generale. \par \par \uc2  Analysts saw demand at the 900-million-zloty auction focused on the longest offered paper the 39-week bill -- in the absence of the benchmark 52-week bill and the 26-week paper. \par \par \uc2  They said however, that after a quiet Monday, foreigners should return on Tuesday buying T-bill on the secondary market. \par \par \uc2  "That is also why Polish banks are likely to boost bids at the primary tender, because they hope to sell it on the market later in the week," one dealer at a foreign bank here said. \par \par \uc2  At the last two auctions foreigners bought a total of about $70 million worth of Polish T-bills. \par \par \uc2  Analysts expected the maximum yield of the 39-week paper at the primary tender unchanged at 21.10 percent. \par \par \uc2  They said small foreign presence at the Monday tender should also keep the local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market quiet, but some expected zloty swings on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "Monday is likely to be quiet because of the London holiday, but some foreign T-bill buying is likely to stir up trade on Tuesday, though much depends on how the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will take it," said Krzysztof Kozuchowski of Bank Handlowy SA. \par \par \uc2  This week the National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped in heavily with estimated $80-90 million \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday to stop the zloty's rise above three percent from the parity rate -- the centre of the daily 14-petrect trading band. \par \par \uc2  The NBP buying was the first in weeks as the bank let the zloty rally whenever foreigners were buying Polish paper to diminish their profit with an unfavourable exchnage rate. \par \par \uc2  The bank has said it was not keen on short-term foreign investment in Polish paper because the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows created problems in managing its money supply. \par \par \uc2  The Wednesday \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  set the zloty back to about 2.5 percent from parity and dealers said foreign investor withdrawal from the sagging Warsaw bourse kept the market bearish on the zloty on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The NBP fixed the Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 2.6935 against the dollar down from 2.6680 a week ago and 1.7455 against the mark, also down from 1.7430 last Friday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expected demand at the Tuesday 374 million zloty one-year floating bond auction to be under 250 million zlotys, because the ministry's decision to remove the five-percent- above-inflation premium from the paper made it unattractive. \par \par \uc2  "With the premium gone, banks are likely to bid with deep discounts, at 96.5-98.5, because the paper is less attractive than a 52-week T-bill," said Pawel Dudzinski of Pekao SA. \par \par \uc2  At the last tender of the paper, which has a coupon equal to year-on-year inflation two months before maturity, the minimum price was 96.67 and the average was 99.57. \par \par \uc2  -- Warsaw Newsroom +48 22 653 9700 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020040127ds5o00ccb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\