\par\par \b \uc2  Outlook for Polish T-bills mixed after April CPI. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jan Strupczewski \par \uc2 531 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Mai 1996\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WARSAW, May 17 (Reuter) - Poland's April inflation data is likely to set off a price tug-of-war at the Monday primary T-bill tender as investors were mixed as to how the figures should influence yields in the near-term, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The Central Statistical Office said on Friday consumer prices grew 2.2 percent in April after a 1.5 percent rise in March taking the year-on-year index down to 20.3 percent from March's 20.4 percent. \par \par \uc2  "The April data could be better, but it is not bad either -- the trend, after all, is down in year-on-year terms," said Pawel Wolczynski of ING bank. \par \par \uc2  "I would expect the benchmark maximum yield to ease slightly to 21.00 or 20.99 percent from 21.05 at the last tender, with the average maybe down to 20.95 percent from 20.98," he said. \par \par \uc2  But others said the larger-than-expected April price rise was unlikely to inspire banks to pay more for the paper as the government inflation target of 17 percent in December became less and less realistic. \par \par \uc2  "The inflation figures were rather uninspiring and I would see the benchmark average yield up at about 21.00 percent," said Wojciech Kowalczyk of Bank Handlowy SA. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said foreign investors could enter the auction at a level similar to last Monday, when they placed an estimated $70 million in Polish paper. \par \par \uc2  They expected total demand at the one-billion zloty auction to be about 1.5 billion zlotys. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think CPI figures, however they are interpreted, will keep foreigners away for long -- if they don't show up in large number on Monday, they will come at the next tender," said Pawel Gajewski of Creditanstalt SA. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the expected foreign entry in Polish T-bills should keep the zloty bullish early in the week. \par \par \uc2  They said the National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the zloty because it wanted to deter short-term foreign investment in Polish paper to limit the inflationary impulse from fast \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserve growth. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to cut foreign profit on Polish T-bills by letting the zloty rise, so I would not expect \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  early in the week," said Creditanstalt's Gajewski. \par \par \uc2  However, some dealers said the dollar's expected rise on world market's might make any \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action unnecessary. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar is seen rising to about 1.54 marks next week, which could take the zloty down to about up to 2.6800 against the dollar," said Marcin Trzykowski of Bank Pekao SA. @ \par \par \uc2  Analysts said players want to keep the zloty between 2.5 and 2.9 percent from the average weighted dollar and mark parity rate -- the centre of the daily trading band -- sailing between the market's bullish zloty mood and the NBP's favoured level of 2.5 percent. \par \par \uc2  "The zloty is likely to be quoted at about 2.7 percent from the parity rate next week," Pekao's Trzykowski said. \par \par \uc2  -- Warsaw Newsroom +48 22 653 9700 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011018ds5h07vcq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
