\par\par \b \uc2  Zloty drifts sideways vs basket, seen quiet. \b0\par\par\uc2 227 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Mai 1996\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WARSAW, May 17 (Reuter) - The Polish zloty was flat against the basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in early trade and was seen continuing its sideways drift during the day unless the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped in to knock it lower, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  They said there were virtually no client orders to stir up trade on the moribund market and the only transactions done were between banks which anticipated the National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2  would, or would not, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the market today. \par \par \uc2  "The market is very quiet as there are no client orders to give it direction, only small specualtion among banks," said Krzysztof Kozuchowski of Bank Handlowy SA. \par \par \uc2  At 0910 GMT the zloty was quoted at 2.6680/87 against the dollar, flat from 2.6670/90 at opening and Thursday. It was at 1.7440/46 against the mark, down from 1.7425/40 at opening and 1.7378/83 on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The zloty was at 2.70 percent from the average weighted dollar and mark parity rates for today -- the centre of the daily 14-percent trading band. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers expected the NBP could move to weaken the zloty to previously favoured levels of about 2.5 percent from parity, taking advantage of the fact that it would not have to go against a market trend. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011018ds5h07v8k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
