\par\par \b \uc2  Polish zloty gains in light dlr-selling, NBP quiet. \b0\par\par\uc2 330 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Mai 1996\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WARSAW, May 13 (Reuter) - The Polish zloty firmed against the basket amid a slight intraday \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -selling trend which did not set off a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Although the National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2  checked rates, it didn't buy and let the zloty inch up amid a light dollar-selling trend," said Malgorzata Pantow of Pekao SA. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  probably refrained from an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  despite the zloty's strong position, at 2.79 from parity, because it wanted to make Polish T-bills more expensive for foreign investors entering today's primary tender. \par \par \uc2  The zloty closed at 2.6612/18 to the dollar, down from Friday's 2.6470/80 and at 1.7359/66 to the mark, up from last week's 1.7449/58. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said today's estimated $70 million worth of foreign T-bill purchases would likely propell the zloty upward on Tuesday and possibly set off NBP \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  On the money market overnight rates rose one point to 17.0/19.0 percent and tomorrow/next rates rose to 18.0/20.0 percent following today's 1.68 billion zloty drainage -- at which the maximum accepted rate held flat at 21.60 percent. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expected the NBP's cuts in the reverse-repo rate last week would boost banks' demand for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's new, paperless NBP-bills, due to be launched this week, especially for the longest 39-week and 52-week NBP- bills. \par \par \uc2  On the secondary T-bill market 52-week paper bought today at an average yield of 20.98 percent was quoted at 21.05/20.80 percent. Foreign investors were absent from secondary trade but dealers expected them to be active on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said that a firm floor on T-bill yields at about 21 percent was formed by pessimistic inflation expectations. \par \par \uc2  -- Wojciech Moskwa +48 22 653 9700 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011018ds5d07w6n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
