\par\par \b \uc2  NBP CURBS ZLOTY RISE VS MARK AND DOLLAR, O/N DOWN. \b0\par\par\uc2 410 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Dezember 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WARSAW, Dec 27 (Reuter) - The National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  mark buying curbed the zloty's run against the dollar and the mark, and established resistance for the Polish unit at about 2.5 percent from the parity rate, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "The market pushed the zloty higher than the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wanted, so it moved to stop the Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's climb," said Bogdan Stepkowski of ING bank in Warsaw. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said, however, that the resistance could be tested again as supply of hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from foreigners planning to buy Polish T-bills was likely to pick up following last week's 2.5 percent appreciation of the parity zloty rate. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said foreigners were likely to be attracted to the T-bills by an impending interest rate cut, expected in late January or February, and by expextations that last week's six percent revaluation of parity rates -- the centre of the 14-percent trading band -- could lead to a further zloty boost. \par \par \uc2  At 0945 GMT, the zloty was quoted at 2.4645/60 to the dollar, just up at offer from opening's 2.4650/80 and stronger than Friday's of 2.4700/50. It was at 1.7197/15 to the mark, up from 1.7200/25 at opening and from 1.7130/60 last week. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought marks on the domestic interbank market at 1.7215 and 1.7220 zlotys. \par \par \uc2  "Foreigners might be promted to enter into Polish T-bills because of widespread expectations of a rate cut and a strong zloty," said Wlodzimierz Berski of Bank Handlowy SA. \par \par \uc2  However, dealers said foregin interest on the secondary T-bill was not significantly larger than usual in early trade, even though it could pick up as the London market revs up. \par \par \uc2  On the money market, overnight rates eased another 0.20 point to 24.00/24.50 percent, a full point lower than the one-day repo rate, as the market remained cash-heavy ahead of today's NBP open market committee meeting, which could cut the one-day repo rate, now at 25.5 percent. \par \par \uc2  Tomorrow/next rates fell 0.35 point to 24.50/25.00 percent, but were seen edging up after today's 800 million zloty reverse repo offer and 400 million zloty NBP-bill sale, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  -- Wojciech Moskwa +48 22 653 9700 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020040130drcr003ac\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
