\par\par \b \uc2  Zloty in dlr footsteps, mkt calm after NBP action. \b0\par\par\uc2 313 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 November 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WARSAW, Nov 28 (Reuter) - The Polish zloty retracted morning losses against the dollar and gains against the mark in active trade, closing near opening levels after closely following the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's swings during the day. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said turbulent dollar/mark trade caused the zloty to strengthen and triggered the first \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  mark-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in weeks, reaffirming the bank's past policy of keeping the zloty within 6.1 percent of the daily parity rate. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a continuation of the National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy... nothing has changed," said Bogdan Stepkowski of ING bank in Warsaw. \par \par \uc2  The zloty closed at 2.5013/23 to the dollar, down from Monday's 2.4950/70. It was at 1.7405/15 to the mark, up from 1.7425/45 yesterday. The NBP bought marks at 1.7430. \par \par \uc2  Despite the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hitting all-time lows against the dollar at 2.5050 during the day, players refrained from buying zlotys. But dealers said banks' were likely to have bought large amounts of zlotys at the NBP fixing. \par \par \uc2  On the money market, one-day rates inched up from lows of 25.00 percent in afternoon trade, as banks became cautious of letting out cash before the end of the monthly required reserve period on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expected overnight rates to ease tomorrow, as banks were likely to find cash after last minute position squaring. \par \par \uc2  "Demand at recent drainages and sales indicate that there is money on the market, so rates are likely to fall tomorrow," said Malgorzata Sikora of ING bank. \par \par \uc2  The secondary T-bill market focused on one-year paper, but trade was light. Some action was also noticed in 91-day NBP-bills, with most transactions being client-driven. \par \par \uc2  -- Wojciech Moskwa +48 22 653 9700 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103drbs019jq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
