\par\par \b \uc2  NBP  \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION \highlight\uc2  BOOSTS ZLOTY TRADE, IMPACT LIMITED. \b0\par\par\uc2 333 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 November 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WARSAW, Nov 9 (Reuter) - The National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's early mark buying \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  sparked a bout of activity on the otherwise quiet market but trade quickly subsided after banks squared positions, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the NBP purchases were of "a regular market size" at 1.7362 zlotys and 1.7364 zlotys which also set the cross dollar/zloty level at about 2.4710/20. \par \par \uc2  They said however, that unless the NBP \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  surplus on the market was likely to induce some dollar selling later today which could take the dollar/zloty to 2.4700 or lower. \par \par \uc2  "There seems to be no single reason for the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , because there was no firm trend on the market when we learned about it," said Marek Golebiewski of PBK SA. \par \par \uc2  The mark-buying, the first \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in weeks, came when the zloty was at 6.06 and 6.05 percent from the central mark parity rate for today at 1.8482 zlotys. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to stabilise the market for the day although the underlying \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  selling mood might help the zloty gain some 10 to 20 basis points against the dollar," said Pawel Gajewski of Creditanstalt SA. \par \par \uc2  On the money market overnight rates eased another 0.06 point from early levels to 25.48/25.54 percent on a net inflow of 229 million zlotys from reverse repo buy-back. \par \par \uc2  Tomorrow/next rates were also offered 0.07 point down at 25.58 percent with no bids, signalling the market expected the cash surplus to hold tomorrow. \par \par \uc2  "There is a lot of cash on the market and nobody wants to pay for it more than 25.48 percent," said Piotr Korcz of Bank Handlowy SA. Dealers said the secondary T-bill market was quiet with only one foreign investor seeking about 10 million zlotys worth of the 26-week paper.-- Jan Strupczewski +48 22 653 9700 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020040129drb900spa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
