\par\par \b \uc2  Polish money rise slows on NBP draining - analysts. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jan Strupczewski \par \uc2 483 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Oktober 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WARSAW, Oct 10 (Reuter) - Slower growth of Polish money supply in September despite fast growing hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves shows the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is successful in sterilising the market and points to non-monetary sources of inflation, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Money supply grew 1.2 percent in September after a 3.3 percent rise in August while official reserves rose $1.245 billion to $12.99 billion in September after a $780 million growth in August. \par \par \uc2  Monthly inflation increased last month to about three percent from 0.4 percent in August and 0.9 percent in July. \par \par \uc2  "The high reserves growth in September and a slowdown in money supply at the same time shows the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  copes very well with sterilising the market," said Maciej Krzak, chief economist at ING bank in Warsaw. \par \par \uc2  "This also undermines the argument of the finance ministry that inflation is mainly fuelled by money supply which slipped out of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's control," he said. \par \par \uc2  The finance ministry has said inflation is mainly driven by the fast growth of official reserves and the resulting money supply increase. It has encouraged the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to both revalue the zloty and lower interest rates, saying this would block the inflow of short-term capital and cooled down exports. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is rejecting both measures at the moment, saying pressure from the reserves should be eliminated by opening up to imports and lowering the budget deficit. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the treasury underestimated such elements as high inflation expectations, rises of government-controlled prices and the low competitiveness of the economy dominated by large, often monopolistic, state-owned firms. \par \par \uc2  The National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's officials said that apart from its money draining \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , money supply was also lower because of seasonal factors. \par \par \uc2  "Although demand for money is higher in September, its turnover in the economy is also much faster, which helps bring down money supply," said Jacek Osinski, deputy head of the monetary policy department. \par \par \uc2  He said large foreign demand for Polish T-bills ahead of the September 18 official rate cut and unfulfilled expectations of a stronger zloty helped sterilise the market. \par \par \uc2  "Foreign purchases reduced the amount of T-bills available to Polish investors and their zloty surplus, together with zlotys created by the growing reserves, could be drained by the NBP instead of flowing out of the system," Osinski said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts also noted the 3.4 percent drop in credit for the budget sector which they said was a result of banks' switch to the attractive outright T-bill sales by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  They said the 1.2 percent fall of non-institutional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposits showed that Poles continued to switch from hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the more attractive zloty deposits despite the two-point rate cut. -- Warsaw Newsroom +48 22 653 9700 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103draa0346y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
