\par\par \b \uc2  NBP holds down zloty vs mark, lets it fall vs dlr. \b0\par\par\uc2 330 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WARSAW, Aug 17 (Reuter) - The Polish zloty fell against the bullish dollar but managed to retrieve some of its losses against the mark before it was stopped by the National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's mark-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  They said the zloty's rise against the German \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , prompted by the dollar's sharp climb on world markets, would have been higher, were it not for the NBP action. \par \par \uc2  "Clearly there was mark-selling pressure, in line with the German unit's fall on world markets, but the NBP stopped the zloty's rise with an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Wojciech Czajkowski of Pomorski Bank Kredytowy SA. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said however, the selling pressure remained strong despite the NBP move, depressing the mark/zloty rate back to the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  level at 1.6670/90 after a brief rebound. \par \par \uc2  They said the today's NBP mark fixing at 1.6675 indicated the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  received mostly mark sell offers from banks which wanted to square positions. \par \par \uc2  "The fixing below the market close indicates the NBP must have received mostly mark sell offers at lower levels signalling continuing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  selling pressure," said Marcin Szereda of Bank Handlowy SA. \par \par \uc2  The zloty closed at 2.4770/90 to the dollar, down from yesterday's 2.4690/10 and 1.6670/90 to the German mark, up from 1.6715/35 yesterday. \par \par \uc2  The National Bank of Poland bought marks at 1.6690 and 1.6695 zlotys, about 5.2 percent from the daily parity rate -- the midpoint of a 14-percent trading band. \par \par \uc2  On the money market overnight rates held flat at 26.55/26.60 percent despite a net inflow of 243 million zlotys from reverse repo buy-back. Tomorrow/next bids rose 0.05 point to 26.55/26.65 percent after a 581 million zloty drainage and 400 million NBP-bill sale.  -- Jan Strupczewski +48 2 625 6303 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr8h04sd2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
